2026-09-07 02:39  autogate.py (new), dispatcher.py 2a99135e -> a549bb26   [BOSS item 1]
  AUTO-GATE: on REPORT_READY the daemon launches mergegate itself (--no-box when no .py proof file,
  and a box item is not launched while another gate runs, so two gates never contend for the box).
  Nothing is gated unless the lane head resolves — measured with git, not assumed.
  AUTO-REWORK: on a FAIL whose Codex row carries a REAL verdict (rc=0, needs-attention/reject), the
  findings are appended VERBATIM to the item file FIRST, then the originating executor is prompted
  with EXECUTOR_STANDING_RULES.md followed by those findings, status becomes `rework`, and ONE
  AUTO_REWORK event is emitted. The item file is written before the prompt because the prompt says
  the block is already there, and a prompt that says so before it is true is a lie the executor acts
  on.
  THE ASYMMETRY IS THE DESIGN: the daemon may hand a FAILING candidate its reviewer's own findings;
  it may NEVER act on a PASS. An unnecessary rework costs one executor turn, an unnecessary merge
  costs trunk. Merges stay BOSS's.
  ESCALATED, never reworked: PASS, DISAGREE, verdict NONE/WALLED/not-run/errored/timed-out, a
  CRASHED gate, the 3rd consecutive auto-fail, and a Codex finding CLASS that repeats from the
  previous rework (severity + the file it names) — the last two being the two ways an item can fail
  to converge, where another automatic round trip is not the answer.
  The decision rules live in autogate.py and are PURE — no posting, writing or launching — so they
  can be tested against recorded gate files with no daemon, server or box.
  Tests: tests/test_autogate.py (the rules; every escalate branch is a case where the machine cannot
  tell a candidate problem from a system problem) and tests/test_autogate_driver.py (what is written
  and posted). The driver's must-bite is BOSS's: a rework prompt is NEVER sent twice for the same
  gate file — keyed on the file's own mtime — because an executor handed the same findings twice
  cannot tell a repeat from a new result. Also covered: a gate file older than the launch is ignored
  (a stale verdict must not be reported as this run's), a failed POST escalates rather than marking
  the item reworked, and a gate that wrote no file in 3h is escalated once and dropped.
