2026-09-07 01:35  dispatcher.py  d6760c7a -> 2a99135e   [BOSS: EXEC-H silent ~5 h]
  ADD check_dead(): an executor holding a DISPATCHED item while producing no text, no running tool
  and no completion for `dead_after_minutes` (default 30) emits STUCK and escalates to BOSS. Once
  per silent stretch — an escalation repeated every 5s is one nobody reads.
  WHY check_stall DID NOT COVER THIS, which is the part worth keeping: once check_stall has posted
  its one RESUME it returns "STALLED (resumed, watching)" on every later tick and never escalates
  again. Watching is not a state anyone is told about, so a session that stays dead after its one
  resume sits forever under a reassuring label. That is how EXEC-H reached ~5 hours.
  Deliberately a SEPARATE and later judgement: check_stall decides whether to resume a TURN (a fast
  judgement about a turn shape), check_dead decides whether to wake a PERSON (a slow one about an
  executor). It fires on the fact that survives every explanation — an item was dispatched and
  nothing has happened since.
  Covers the second shape too: a session that produced NO assistant message at all, aged from our
  own prompt.
  A session mid-compaction is NOT reported dead; COMPACTING owns that case.
  COST: the dedupe check runs BEFORE compaction_active(), which costs an API call — otherwise a
  known-dead session would pay that call every tick to re-learn the same thing. Pinned by a test
  that counts the lookups.
  Test: tests/test_deadexec.py. The CONTROLS carry as much weight as the must-bite, because this
  escalation wakes BOSS: a busy executor mid-tool-call, one producing text, a young empty turn, a
  COMPLETED turn, an ERRORED turn, an executor with no dispatched item, a compaction, and empty or
  None input. An alarm that cries wolf on a healthy executor is one BOSS learns to ignore, which
  costs the real one its signal.
  Suite rc 0, ALL GREEN, 382 PASS.
