2026-09-06 22:35  tests/fixturelog.py (new), all 18 tests/test_*.py
  MARK fixture-driven output, so the only unmarked FAIL or UNWIRED text in a suite log is a real
  one. BOSS, reading a GREEN log: it carried three "=== UNWIRED TEST FILES ===" blocks and several
  bare "FAIL" gate verdicts, every one of them a prop — a gate deliberately driven to fail, a census
  deliberately given an unwired stub. A log in which findings and props are typographically
  identical does not become safe because the exit code is right; it moves the reading cost onto
  whoever skims it, and the one time it matters is the time it is real.
  fixturelog.prefixed() captures a block that runs REAL code and re-emits every line with a
  `[fixture]` prefix (it still emits if the block RAISES — a crash's own output is the most useful
  kind). fixturelog.flat() collapses a check()'s detail to one line, because a multi-line detail
  puts its own tail at column 0 where it reads as output in its own right.
  The helper is deliberately NOT named test_*.py, so the runner's census does not demand a run line.
  PROVEN, not assumed: with an injected real failure the log contains EXACTLY ONE unmarked FAIL line
  and it is the injected one; with the suite green there are none.
  Two defects of mine on the way: a two-substitution loop that re-matched the text it had just
  inserted (both wrappers landed on the first call site), and an inserted `sys.path` line placed
  above the file's own `import sys` — NameError in test_gates_listing. Both caught by running it.

