  They were also wrong in the other direction: two behaviour-preserving refactors of the same call
  (a kwarg, a renamed local) turned test_reportclock RED. A check that is blind to deletion and
  loud on a rename is not measuring the thing it names.
  FIX: each wiring claim is now measured by RUNNING the code.
   - test_missingvenv drives the real _run_proofs with the box lock pointed at a temp directory (the
     real box is never taken) and every subprocess stubbed, and asserts the refusal the GATE emits.
   - test_reportclock drives the real gate over a future-dated report and requires the row in the
     .md it writes, plus that the row is advisory.
   - test_codexwall counts real reviewer calls, and gains the CONTROL that a NON-wall failure is
     called exactly ONCE — without it, a retry-everything policy passes every wall check.
  All four mutations re-run after the fix: the three previously-invisible deletions now go RED, and
  the behaviour-preserving kwarg refactor now stays GREEN.
