## 2026-09-06 21:31 — dispatcher.py: the stale tag must not read a SHARED artifact
md5 84bb074a -> 4fdae015. Semantics correction from the tag's second firing (BOSS): PENDING_STALE
hit EXEC-A2's LIVE REPORT_READY because its item carried an artifact that had LANDED.

Artifacts are SHARED BY DESIGN. Every `A.<x>` audit item shares its artifact with the `B.<x>` build
it audits, and every rework (-r2/-r3/-renumber) shares its parent's; 13 were shared across non-final
items when BOSS measured, 8 across finished/unfinished pairs in the recorded queue. So
`A.012.sequence-ended-event` sitting `reported` under a MERGED `012.sequence-ended-event` is the
CORRECT state, not a stale row, and reading the artifact retires live work under a finished sibling.

New rule: once the item resolves, ITS OWN QUEUE STATUS IS THE SOLE AUTHORITY — the ledger is not
consulted at all. The ledger becomes evidence only when the item does NOT resolve, i.e. the row's
item was renamed or removed from the queue (BOSS renamed ten tonight), and then only against an
artifact RECORDED ON THE ROW; a row with no artifact is left alone rather than guessed at. Rows now
record their artifact as well as their item at both creation sites, which is what makes that path
reachable instead of theoretical.

tests/test_stalerow.py 24/24. The new must-passes use REAL items from the recorded queue rather than
invented ones: `A.012.sequence-ended-event` (reported, artifact MERGED) stays untagged, and
`B.010.ci-collection-floor` (dispatched, shared artifact) stays untagged whether that artifact is
HELD or LANDED. A fixture assertion checks the queue still shares artifacts across finished and
unfinished items at all — if that ever stops being true these must-passes would pass vacuously.

## 2026-09-06 21:28 — dispatcherctl.sh clear + dispatcher.py: a sanctioned way to drop ONE row
dispatcher.py md5 fd58bbe9 -> 84bb074a; dispatcherctl.sh gains `clear <row-key> "<reason>"`.

BOSS had no way to clear a row that is real but finished with, and hand-editing was not one — and
that is the design constraint, not a preference: `pending` lives in the daemon's in-memory state and
is rewritten by save_state() every tick, so an edit from outside would be silently overwritten
within five seconds. So ctl writes a REQUEST and the daemon does the removal:
- ctl validates against the board (pending.json): exact msg_id or session first, then a unique
  substring. Zero matches or more than one is REFUSED, and it prints the candidates or the whole
  board so the next attempt can name one exactly. A clear with no reason is refused too — an
  unexplained deletion of a row BOSS was asked to rule on is the thing this must not enable.
  ctl never edits pending.json (asserted).
- the daemon consumes the request at tick start, RE-VALIDATES it (the board ctl read may be seconds
  old; a row that has since changed is not removed on a stale match — PENDING_CLEAR_REFUSED says
  so), removes exactly that row, emits PENDING_CLEARED carrying BOSS's reason, and deletes the
  request so it cannot be applied twice.
`CN` in dispatcherctl.sh is now taken from the environment (defaulting to the real checkout) so the
script itself can be exercised against a temp tree — the verb is worth testing through the script
BOSS actually types, not a re-implementation of it.

Calibration tests/test_clearrow.py, 11/11, hermetic: a temp CN, the real ctl invoked against it, no
daemon, no live state. The clearing case asserts the other rows are BYTE-IDENTICAL afterwards, and
the request file is consumed — with dry=False on purpose, because _rm() is a no-op in dry mode and a
dry run therefore could not prove it (a surviving request would re-apply every tick). Must-refuse:
an ambiguous key, a key matching nothing, a missing reason, and a request whose row has changed.

