## 2026-09-06 21:07 — dispatcher.py: a COMPACTION is not a provider-cut turn (BOSS gap 2)
md5 74d6c875 -> cf16a0d8. Rerun: `./dispatcherctl.sh selftest`.

21:03:59, EXEC-D: STALL_ESCALATED fired on a turn that was compacting. An auto-compaction ends the
assistant turn, posts a user message carrying a `compaction` part, and starts a fresh assistant turn
with no parts and no completion — byte-for-byte the shape stall_shape() calls a provider cut. The
test asserts that directly (`stall_shape() really does classify it as a stall`), so the guard is
measured against the thing it prevents rather than against an assumption.

`compaction_active(sid)` reads the newest USER message in a 3-message window and returns its id when
it carries a `compaction` part, or the synthetic `compaction_continue` nudge opencode posts after
one. It is consulted ONLY at the moment a stall would be DECLARED — one extra API call per stall
decision, not one per poll per executor. When it fires: emit COMPACTING once (naming the compaction
message), leave the stall window unfired, post NOTHING, and suppress STALLED_TURN / RESUME /
escalation. A RESUME landing inside a compaction is the failure being avoided: it burns the
session's first post-compaction turn at the moment its context was just rewritten. The episode ends
on the first turn that looks alive, and normal detection resumes there.

Calibration tests/test_compaction.py, 16/16, against fixtures/execd_compaction.json — the real
three-message window. Three assertions are on the FIXTURE (the compaction part is there, the
post-compaction turn really has no parts and no completion, and stall_shape really does bite it),
and the must-bite is kept in the same file: an inert turn whose newest user message is an ordinary
prompt still fires STALLED_TURN and still posts exactly one RESUME. A guard that suppressed both
would pass a test that only checked the first half.

## 2026-09-06 21:04 — mergegate.py: report header time vs the wall clock (advisory)
md5 b5f3fcec -> 7c95c73a. `report_clock_row()`, called on the report the gate already reads.
EXEC-F's header said 21:00 IST at a wall clock of 20:58:52. A header written in the future is a
small thing that poisons big ones: it is the timestamp used to reconstruct what happened in what
order, and every ordering argument quoting it inherits the error. ADVISORY by construction — it
goes through warn(), never rec(), and the test asserts that from the source.
Threshold 60 s, and the number is reasoned rather than picked: a minute-granularity header can
legitimately round UP by up to 59 s, so 60 s is the smallest threshold that cannot fire on rounding
alone; EXEC-F's 68 s is outside it. Reads only the first 30 lines, so a future time quoted in a
report's body is not this finding. Catches a future date on a date-only header too.
An unparseable header is REPORTED ("NOT CHECKED — ... This row is not a pass"), never skipped: a
check that stays silent when it could not read its input is indistinguishable from one that read it
and found nothing.
Calibration tests/test_reportclock.py, 11/11, `now` injected so it does not depend on the clock:
the real 20:58:52/21:00 case fires; 20:58 and a rounded-up 20:59 do not; a future date-only header
fires and today's does not; an unreadable header says NOT CHECKED; a timestamp 40 lines down is not
read; and the body of the function never calls rec().

