detector called msg_077483e5e cut ("inert 123s over 25 polls: no text, no running tool, not
completed") and posted a RESUME. The message was NOT dead: it completed at 20:50:55 carrying a full
PLAN READY. But `last_message()` reads `?limit=1`, and by then the last message was our own resume,
so the completed PLAN READY was never classified, no PLAN_READY event existed anywhere, no plan
review ran, and the executor parked six minutes later on a QUESTION about the review that never came.

Scanning the cut message's parts — the fix as briefed — would NOT have caught this: at stall time
that message had no text at all. The test asserts exactly that, as a negative control, so nobody
later mistakes the scan for the fix. What loses the event is reading only the last message.

Three changes, in order of what they actually buy:
1. `recover_missed_marker()` — on a tick where the last message is a user turn AND we have a
   `stall_resumed` record for that session, look past it at the last 5 messages for a completed
   assistant message this daemon never handled that carries a line-start PLAN READY / REPORT READY.
   Emits the real event marked RECOVERED and starts the plan review for the dispatched item. Bounded
   by design: no resume on record means no scan at all, and each message is recovered once
   (`state["recovered"]`). A recovered REPORT READY is logged but NOT queued for BOSS as a new row —
   it may be the report the executor is about to re-post under the resume, and two rows for one
   report is its own defect.
2. STALL_RESUME_PROMPT now tells the executor to re-post a PLAN/REPORT READY block verbatim if it
   posted one in the cut turn, and says why (the dispatcher reads only the last message).
3. `check_stall()` emits CUT_TURN_MARKER when the cut message already carried a marker — the
   partially-landed case, which the BUSY branch never classifies because it never reads text.

Calibration: tests/test_cutmarker.py, 13/13, against fixtures/execd_cut_turn.json — the real
message, with the dispatcher's own RESUME as the last entry. Two of the assertions are on the
FIXTURE itself (the last message really is the resume; the PLAN READY really did complete), because
a fixture that has drifted out of the bug's shape proves nothing about the fix.

## 2026-09-06 20:52 — mergegate.py: merge preflight, reviewers off the box wait, whole portal suite (units 2, 2b, 3)
md5 f089cc18 -> b5f3fcec. Three units in one write because they are one file; each is independent.

UNIT 2 — `run_merge_preflight()`, a HARD check (§5.4). Every other check in this gate measures the
LANE, and a migration number is only unique in the tree it lands in: a lane that legitimately took
247 in isolation collides with a trunk that took 247 elsewhere, and nothing lane-only can see it —
tonight a second 247 would have PASSed. core/db.py:518 refuses a gap as hard as a duplicate, so the
collision stops the runner. Built in a disposable integration worktree off trunk, `git merge
--no-commit --no-ff`, always aborted and removed, so neither trunk nor the lane is touched; a lane
in a different repo (the Codex checkouts) is fetched first. FAILs on (a) same migration number with
different filenames, naming both, (b) duplicate numbers in the merged tree, (c) merge conflicts,
listing the files. ★'s correction is load-bearing: `comm -12` over the two trees' numbers always
reports a collision because lane and trunk share 001..248 by construction — the question is per
number, do the trees hold different filenames. Proofs are NOT run on the merged tree (box cost;
separate decision). Calibration test_preflight.py, real git repos in a temp dir: 12/12 — clean lane
PASSes, collision FAILs naming both files, conflict FAILs naming the file, a number shared by both
trees is not a collision, and after every case the integration worktree is gone and trunk is clean.

UNIT 2b — the Codex review now starts alongside agy, BEFORE the box wait, instead of after
run_proofs. The review is read-only and needs nothing scarce; the box is the scarce thing. ~10 min
off each gate's wall clock. The thread does not call rec(): `state["ok"] = state["ok"] and passed`
is a read-modify-write and a lost update in a gate verdict is not worth saving a join for — it
parks its result and the main thread records it, so row order in the .md is unchanged. Fail-CLOSED
is unchanged and now also covers a thread still alive at 1800 s. .codex.txt is still written the
moment the review finishes, so a later crash still leaves it on disk. Preflight deliberately stays
BEFORE both reviewers: it is seconds, it fails fast on a candidate that cannot merge at all, and it
keeps its `git worktree` work off the same repo while a review is running.

UNIT 3 — portal proofs run the WHOLE vitest suite (plus typecheck) whenever any portal/** file is
in the candidate diff, named .tsx proofs or not. The row ran only the item's selected files — 25
tests — so 011's and 014a's merges both passed while src/lib/permissions.test.ts was red on trunk:
the portal role table was missing approval:decide and five asset.* permissions that auth.py grants,
paid features absent from customer navigation, and no gate looked. The suite is ~1.5 s/file and
box-free, so selecting files bought nothing and cost a merge. The row now names which mode it ran
("WHOLE SUITE" vs "selected [...]"), because a row that does not say what it ran cannot be audited.
test_portal_row.py (subprocess stubbed): 8/8 — whole mode passes no file args, typecheck still
runs, a portal/** file in the diff promotes the row even with no .tsx proof, and an item with
neither is unchanged.

