## 2026-09-06 20:45 — lockwatch.py: OWNER_MOVED_IN_PLACE was labelling legitimate handoffs
Fired live at 20:36:37 on exec-e-Q7r2proof. Ground truth (BOSS, 20:37:12): EXEC-C acquired by
atomic mkdir at 20:36:22 for a single test and released; EXEC-E's `sleep 180; if mkdir` succeeded
at 20:36:34 — impossible unless the dir was gone; EXEC-C pid 18495 dead; census count 1; EXEC-C's
next attempts print HELD. A release and a re-acquire that both fell inside one 5 s poll, so the
watcher never saw the dir absent. The detector already distinguished "recreated" from "rewritten"
in its MESSAGE but not in its LABEL, and the label is what gets acted on: this one would have
justified a false abort.

Fix: a bumped dir mtime is proof a real mkdir happened, which is proof the dir was gone, which is
the mutual exclusion holding — that branch now fires RACE_CHURN (handoff, informational, "do not
abort on it") and marks the (a) episode key so the same episode cannot also report as a breach.
OWNER_MOVED_IN_PLACE is reserved for the case where no mkdir can have occurred: owner text changed
with the dir mtime UNCHANGED. The cold-start skew inference is unchanged. Poll interval NOT
shortened — a 1 s poll still misses a sub-second handoff and costs CPU on a shared box.

Calibration: new MUST-NOT-BITE fixture (a'') built from this event's real timestamps and owner
tokens, asserting OWNER_MOVED_IN_PLACE=0 and RACE_CHURN=1; the existing must-bite (a) with the dir
mtime held still asserts exactly 1. `--selftest` PASS, 7/7 (must-pass, a, a', a'' must-not-bite,
b, c, d).

Measured on events.log: of 44 OWNER_MOVED_IN_PLACE events ever emitted, 39 say "bumped to" (all
handoffs, now RACE_CHURN), 4 say "unchanged at" (the real shape) and 1 is the cold-start inference.
The label was wrong on ~89% of the events it produced.

## 2026-09-06 20:4x — dispatcher.py + dispatcherctl.sh: four board-honesty fixes (BOSS spec, post-reboot)
Unit-tested by scratch test_fixes.py (30 assertions) against COPIES of the 20:37 live
queue.json / pending.json / roster.json; verified with `dispatcher.py --once --dry-run`.

1. dep_ok() resolved deps against the LEDGER by ARTIFACT name only. Deps written as ITEM ids
   (B.011.port-row-decoding, B.010.trunk-ruff-clean, and 7 more in tonight's queue) match no
   ARTIFACT line, so a finished dependency read as unfinished — a permanent block the board drew
   as an ordinary wait. Now: ledger artifact -> queue item by id -> queue item by artifact (and
   that item's artifact status in the ledger). A dep matching none of them still blocks (dispatching
   on an unknown dependency is worse) but is emitted once an hour as DEP_UNRESOLVABLE and shown as
   `<dep> [DEP_UNRESOLVABLE]` in blocked_on, so it can never again look like waiting. Both callers
   updated (eligible_item, codex_tick).

2. Stale/dead ERROR rows (§5.2). Three of tonight's four pending rows were unreachable history:
   (a) prune_superseded_error() — an ERROR row whose msg_id is no longer the session's last message
       and whose newer message is clean is deleted with a CLEARED event. Only QUEUED/BUSY cleared a
       row before, so an ERROR followed by any other clean turn sat there forever.
   (b) prune_offroster() — pending is keyed by session id and the tick only visits sessions IN the
       roster, so a row for a retired/replaced session (EXEC-A retired 04:36; EXEC-B's and EXEC-E's
       pre-rotation sessions) had no clear path at all. Two guards: nothing is pruned when the
       explicit roster is empty (config failed to load), and a session must be off the roster for a
       full 10-min grace, so one bad discovery tick costs a delay instead of a row.
   (c) A non-retryable 400 that survives both escalation levels is classified DEAD: one
       EXECUTOR_DEAD event, one escalation naming it as a session to retire or replace, then
       silence; the board says `DEAD:ERROR non-retryable 400 ... session unusable, replace it`
       instead of `idle:ERROR waiting for BOSS`. age_and_escalate also stops escalating OFF_ROSTER
       rows entirely — the first staged dry-run fired three DEAD escalations for rows that were
       simply on their way out.

3. Circuit-breaker feed holds. hold/<EXEC>.feed said "BOSS: answer its QUESTIONs then delete this
   file" and nothing deleted it; clear_feed_holds_for_answered() only fires when a queued item
   carries answered_at, so an executor that merely resumed stayed unfed (two sat 17 h).
   release_hold() — called on CLEARED — now removes the .feed hold too, resets the breaker's park
   window and emits HOLD_RELEASED. write_pending() also appends " [FEED HELD]" to any held
   executor's board line, so a live hold is never invisible.

4. dispatcherctl.sh status now reports the STANDALONE lockwatch from
   test-logs/driver/lockwatch.pid: ALIVE with the pid, or "pidfile says N but NO such process — box
   lock is UNWATCHED", or no pidfile at all. STOP silences the daemon's own poll_lockwatch(), so
   status must not imply monitoring that is not running (restart recipe Step 1b).

