`dispatcherctl.sh answer <item-id> "<text>" [--relayed]` — post the reply and un-park in ONE step.

BOSS, on his own fourth silent failure of the night: "I act on the system through a side channel and
leave its record untouched." A QUESTION parks its item by design so the question waits for him. He
answered four by direct prompt_async and never cleared the parks; `parked` is not gateable, so
EXEC-J built a finished artifact whose REPORT READY was skipped at 04:57 with "holds no dispatched,
rework or reported item".

ORDER IS THE DESIGN. Post first; the row moves only if it succeeded. A failed post leaves the row
PARKED, emits ANSWER_FAILED and escalates — a row un-parked before a failed post says the executor
is building when nobody told it anything, the same drift running the other way. Both directions are
mutated.

Refusals name what the item IS, at both layers (ctl for the fast no, the daemon again against the
live queue, because ctl's read is a snapshot). Not parked names the status; unknown id suggests near
matches; no executor recorded says so. The request file is consumed on a refusal too, or every tick
replays it.

RELAY LANES (WORKER-1/WORKER-2/BOSS, no opencode session): a bare `answer` is REFUSED, because
un-parking on a post that never happened is the bug this verb exists to prevent. `--relayed` un-parks
on BOSS's word and the event records that no post was attempted.

A park now records `parked_from` and `answer` restores it. Rows parked before that existed restore
to `dispatched` with the event saying the value was ASSUMED — a guess presented as a reading is how
the next drift starts. The executor's QUESTION pending row is withdrawn, or it escalates at 10 and
20 minutes for a question already answered.

SECOND FINDING, FOUND WHILE BUILDING THIS: `dispatcherctl.sh gate` HAS NEVER WORKED. It read
$CN/dispatcher/queue.json; no such file has ever existed (the live queue is $STATE/queue.json), so
every hand-launched gate died at "cannot read ...: [Errno 2]" and exited 2. Invisible because the
daemon's automatic path is unaffected — every gate tonight was AUTO_GATE — and because
test_manualgate BUILT ITS FIXTURE AT THE SAME WRONG PATH. The test was green about a verb that could
not run. Fixed, with a control: a queue present only at the old path must refuse, not launch.

Also deleted a `check(..., True)` I had just written in test_answer.py. A check whose condition is
the literal True cannot fail. It was unfinished scaffolding and it would have read as coverage.

