THE GATE WAS MANUFACTURING ITS OWN FAILURES. Two separate causes, one theme: the gate's own
scheduling was showing up as the candidate's defect.

(1) PORTAL CONCURRENCY. BOSS measured 27 gate portal runs on 2026-09-07: the 24 that ran alone were
ALL GREEN, the 3 that overlapped another gate's portal run were ALL RED — the overlap set and the
red set were the same set, no exceptions either way. Control: trunk's own portal suite alone is 97
files / 845 tests / exit 0 (05:35); the same tree run twice concurrently produced 5 and 4 failures,
different tests each time, all wait-timeout shaped. A known-green tree goes red purely from
concurrency, and each invented red costs an executor a full rework round.

Portal proofs were classified box-free, so the daemon's one-gate-at-a-time deferral never covered
them, and the rate was ~one collision per rework round at 14 executors — it scales with how WELL the
lanes are doing.

Fix: a portal lock, same shape as the box lock, around the PORTAL LEG ONLY (BOSS's own sizing: the
other checks are unaffected by concurrency). It queues rather than fails. A gate that cannot get it
within 40 min records NOT RUN, never FAIL. Two controls keep it honest in both directions: a lock
whose owner PROCESS IS GONE and is older than 30 min is broken and said to be broken, and a lock
whose owner is ALIVE is never stolen however old. Start/end/duration/queue-wait now ride in the row
— BOSS had to reconstruct timelines from log filenames and mtimes, which dies at the first rotation.
The overlap disclosure is kept even though the lock should make it impossible: a lock can be broken
as dead or bypassed, and if that WARN row ever appears it is itself a defect to chase.

Deliberately NOT at the daemon's launch level: the lock is inside mergegate, so it also holds for
hand-launched gates and anything bypassing the daemon.

(2) A QUEUED GATE WAS INVISIBLE, AND IT COST TWO HEALTHY GATES. BOSS reported the agy leg hanging
for 42 and 28 minutes. It was not agy. `packed.txt` is written AFTER gatereview2.review() RETURNS,
so its presence PROVES the leg finished; both items declare .py proofs, so both take the box; the
box lock's owner file named EXEC-G pid=95921 from 05:13:48 and was still held; and the box wait is a
30 s poll with a 3600 s cap. Both gates were 43 and 28 minutes into a legitimate wait. Every wall
BOSS cited was working — nothing outlived them because nothing was hanging.

The defect was that none of this was on the wire: no event, no file, and the .md is written only at
the end, so "waiting for the box" and "hung" were the same observation. BOSS reached the only
conclusion the evidence allowed. Now: GATE_WAITING_FOR_BOX at minute 0 and every 10 min after —
naming the box owner, saying the gate is ALIVE and queued, and saying that killing it discards a
Codex review already paid for — plus GATE_GOT_BOX with the queue time and GATE_GAVE_UP_ON_BOX at
the cap. Repetition is the point: one line at minute zero is off the screen by minute forty.

Corollary for BOSS's numbers: his post-Codex distribution (median 0.0 min across 53 gates, three
tails at 35.3/33.0/24.5) is almost certainly measuring BOX QUEUE TIME, not agy speed. Re-derive it
once GATE_GOT_BOX exists; if the tails survive a short queue, agy really is slow on large diffs.

(3) A REPORT ON A PARKED ROW IS RAISED, not swallowed as an IDLE_ACK saying "holds no dispatched,
rework or reported item" — false about this case, since the item exists and is parked. Third
occurrence on 2026-09-07 (EXEC-J 04:57, EXEC-H 05:10, one earlier). It escalates naming the item and
the exact `dispatcherctl.sh answer` command, and tells the executor its work is safe and not to
resend. Control: an executor holding genuinely nothing still takes the ordinary path.

APPLY NOTE. mergegate.py is gate-stamped and four gates were running, so safe_apply refused —
correctly. With a collision per rework round the box is rarely gate-free, so a watcher polled every
15 s and took the first window (06:11:51, "no gate was running at the instant of the write"). It
never killed anything, never paused the board, and never bypassed safe_apply. The unstamped half
(dispatcher.py, test_answer.py) landed earlier at 05:50:01 because a running gate never loads it.

