The conjunction: owner names a pid AND that pid is dead (unnamed is not dead); lock older than
30 min; NO pytest anywhere; token unchanged across a 10 s recheck; and (added here) the owner read
once more immediately before the unlink — a test that injected a re-acquire at exactly that point
found nothing looking.

(2) COUNTING PYTEST IS TWO STAGES, AND BOTH ONE-STAGE VERSIONS ARE WRONG.
BOSS hit both. `ps -eo args | grep -c "[-]m pytest"` returned SIX with ONE pytest running: the other
five were Codex processes whose PROMPT TEXT quotes `./.venv/bin/python -m pytest`, because our own
handoff instructions contain it and the whole prompt sits in argv. Failure: the lock is never
released. Wasteful. Filtering on `ps -eo comm` instead: the multi-column form truncates comm to 16
chars, so a live run reads `./.venv/bin/pyth` and a `python$` regex returns 0 while a pytest is
alive. Failure: a second Postgres cluster on top of a live one. Dangerous — and it is what you reach
for after the first burns you.

Correct: candidates by command line, then each candidate's own comm via `ps -p <pid> -o comm=`,
which as the ONLY requested field is untruncated. Measured live: 6 candidates, 1 real. I had shipped
wrong-way-1 in the staged version; it never reached disk.

THE GENERAL SHAPE, and it is not about ps: OUR OWN INSTRUCTION TEXT IS NOW LONG ENOUGH AND SPECIFIC
ENOUGH TO MATCH OUR OWN MONITORING GREPS. Four argv matchers exist here. safe_apply, dispatcher's
gates_running and precheck fail SAFE (a phantom refuses an apply or defers a gate). checkpoint's
failed DANGEROUS — its ghost-gate check faults a `gated` row only when there is no gate file AND no
gate process, so a prompt quoting "dispatcher/mergegate.py" (this file's own reports do) would make
a phantom gate SILENCE the abandoned-row check. Fixed the same way.

(3) A RESTART IS NO LONGER "DESTROY EVERY IN-FLIGHT GATE AND AUDIT".
The running daemon (pid 36697, started 06:18:02) was executing code older than dispatcher.py's mtime
(06:24:18): the PROGRESS_STOP fix was applied, green, and NOT RUNNING. Symptom: EXEC-M idle for 27
minutes holding nothing while an eligible item was queued to it. Staleness is self-concealing.

  * `stop` kills a RESOLVED pid. It was `pkill -f "dispatcher/dispatcher.py"` — a pattern kill on a
    shared box, the one thing every executor is told never to do, matching any shell that mentions
    the path.
  * `restart` refuses while children are live, naming each with its elapsed time, and needs --force.
    `--dry-run` exists so the NO-children branch is testable without stopping a real daemon.
  * `status` reports STALE when dispatcher.py is newer than the daemon's start.

On BOSS's option 1 (children surviving a restart): MEASURED, and it is already half true. Both spawn
sites use start_new_session=True and the live children carry their own pgids (daemon 36697, gate
41487, codex 47713/47865), so a process-group kill does not reach them. What is unproven is whether
`launchctl bootout` attributes by job and takes them anyway — and testing that means destroying the
jobs in question. Hence the refusal: it makes the answer unnecessary.

