2026-09-08 04:25  D — museadapter.py, the managed launch path for Codex CLI Muse workers (WORKER-2)
  Plan 003 §5. NEW: museadapter.py (a68a1cb0), tests/test_muse.py (f96314a5, 77 checks),
  MUSE_ADAPTER.md (48b63340); tests/run_all.sh wired (427a7a69). Suite 62 files, ALL GREEN.

  run_attempt() is the only entry point and holds every guard, so no caller can forget one. The
  chain, each link refused where nothing has been spent yet: the profile declares a route -> the
  credential exists (checked BY NAME, never by value) -> the flags were accepted -> no failure
  event in the output -> the intended route actually ran -> a terminal result correlated to item,
  attempt and session on a path no earlier run could have written. Exit 0 is nowhere in that chain:
  it is a statement about the process, not about the work, and the pilot's worker exited 0 having
  done nothing.

  Ten outcomes, each pointing a human somewhere different: OK BLOCKED AUTH QUOTA TRANSPORT
  CLI_CONFIG CANCELLED INCOMPLETE ROUTE_MISMATCH NO_ATTEMPT. NO_ATTEMPT is deliberately not AUTH —
  a credential that was never offered was never refused, and calling that an auth failure sends
  somebody to inspect an account that is fine. Only TRANSPORT is retried; QUOTA waits for a clock
  and AUTH waits for a human.

  Routing: Go first, round-robin across all three keys so no one account discovers its limit alone;
  zen permitted as a configured fallback (owner via BOSS 2026-09-08, withdrawing "never zen"). The
  failure condition is "not the profile THIS attempt intended", which catches a Go attempt landing
  on zen AND a zen attempt landing on Go. The route is read back from session_meta (provider) and
  turn_context (model/effort), never from the worker's own report, because the pilot's worker
  reported a model it was not running and because five zen sessions from a scratch workspace that
  is not ours appeared on this machine on 2026-09-08. An unreadable route is UNMEASURED, and a
  completed result on an unmeasured route returns INCOMPLETE: the work may be fine, but the claim
  "this ran on <profile>" is the thing that cannot be made.

  Credentials never leave the child environment: only bool(value) is ever consulted, every other
  account's key is REMOVED from the child even when inherited, and the KEY1 -> KEY_1 spelling gap
  is bridged there rather than by editing the shared key file. The alias map stays even though ★
  has since fixed the file — a file that happens to be correct today is not a guard.

  Mutation score, CALL SITES not helpers (standing rule 2026-09-07): 8 mutations, 8 CAUGHT, 0
  MISSED; source restored and verified against its pre-image md5. Scored: dropping the credential
  refusal, trusting the exit code, dropping the route refusal, dropping the result refusal, making
  result paths reusable so a stale result satisfies a new run, resuming with --last, carrying every
  account's credential into the child, and accepting an unverified route as OK.

  Two defects found in my own code by running the set. (1) session_id_from_jsonl required an id of
  >= 8 characters "because ids are UUIDs"; a short id then read as NO ID AT ALL and the attempt was
  reported INCOMPLETE for the wrong reason — a length floor on a format that belongs to someone
  else buys nothing and hides what it rejects. (2) Three of my checks were themselves broken: a
  secret-leak check whose sentinel was the letter "v" (it matches ordinary prose), a path check
  forbidding ".." inside an already-sanitised basename, and a source-order check that matched
  run_attempt's docstring instead of its body.

  NOT BUILT, named rather than left implicit: the pool that decides concurrency N and reports wait
  reasons (run_attempt is per-attempt and holds no global state, so N concurrent workers already
  work — nothing schedules them); and partial-edit preservation on a killed attempt, which needs
  C's worktree convention first.

