2026-09-08 05:3x  SELECTION POLICY on the spawn path (WORKER-2, after BOSS's grep)
  museadapter.py, dispatcher.py, tests/test_muse.py (113), tests/test_codexroute.py (21),
  MUSE_ADAPTER.md. Suite ALL GREEN.

  BOSS: preference_order and next_profile were called by nothing but their own tests, so the owner's
  instruction — distribute across all three keys, skip a key that has stopped working, fall back to
  zen when Go stalls — was not implemented anywhere the daemon could reach. Landed-and-not-live, one
  layer inside the commit that fixed exactly that shape.

  select_profile(slot, item, cfg, state) replaces slot_route, which is DELETED rather than kept: a
  helper the daemon does not call is the thing being fixed, and two selectors invite the wrong one.
  Order: an item's own profile (honoured even when held, because someone asked for that one) ->
  nothing configured means LEGACY -> a `rotate` token means round-robin over the healthy Go profiles
  -> a profile NAME pins, unless held, in which case the dispatch is stepped over -> zen only when
  EVERY Go profile is held. Every branch returns a NOTE, and the daemon logs it whenever the choice
  differs from the configured one: a slot silently running on a key its config does not name is the
  failure this layer exists to prevent.

  `last` is persisted in self.state["muse"], so rotation advances across ticks. In a local it would
  restart at the head every time and hand every dispatch to muse-go-1 — proved by a mutation.

  Health has three classes because they need three answers. AUTH and a missing credential are held
  UNTIL A HUMAN clears them (retrying just refuses again); QUOTA is held until a CLOCK
  (quota_hold_minutes, default 60 — the key is spent, not broken); TRANSPORT is not held at all,
  being the one worth retrying. The spawn path holds a profile whose credential it cannot find; the
  reap holds one whose log reports AUTH or QUOTA.

  A DEFECT MY OWN CHECK CAUGHT MID-WRITE: the first cut fell through to rotation when nothing was
  configured, which would have put every unconfigured slot on Muse as a side effect of wiring the
  policy — the same silent re-route the default was chosen to avoid, reintroduced by the fix for
  something else. The check that caught it existed only because I had written it for the earlier
  wiring.

  Mutations: 9 of 9 CAUGHT across both files, sources restored and md5-verified. Never rotate;
  ignore held profiles; hold AUTH on a clock; hold TRANSPORT; reach zen before Go is exhausted;
  rotate an unconfigured slot; stop persisting `last`; route without selecting; do not hold a
  refused credential.

  NOT LIVE YET, and it is a config line, not code: roster.json pins CODEX-1 to muse-go-1 and CODEX-2
  to muse-go-2. Pinned slots do not rotate and muse-go-3 is never selected. Rotation needs
  `codex_routes: {CODEX-1: rotate, CODEX-2: rotate}`. roster.json is not mine to edit.

2026-09-08 05:5x  Ten-slot prerequisites: stagger, REFUSED, and the restart path (WORKER-2)
  dispatcher.py, dispatcherctl.sh, tests/test_codexroute.py (28), tests/test_ctlself.py (NEW, 7),
  tests/run_all.sh. Suite 64 files, ALL GREEN.

  STAGGER. The owner has chosen 2 Astra + 8-10+ Muse slots, speed first. Every spawn does a
  `git worktree add` AND a `uv sync --frozen` before the model is ever called, so ten simultaneous
  spawns make SETUP the bottleneck and all ten pay for each other. codex_spawn now DEFERS a spawn
  that arrives within codex_spawn_stagger_seconds (default 20) of the last successful one — defers,
  not refuses: the item stays exactly where it was and the next tick takes it. A stagger that broke
  items would be worse than none. The clock starts when a spawn SUCCEEDS, so a failing spawn cannot
  hold the queue behind it. 0 disables it.

  REFUSED IS NOT FAILED. A quota or auth refusal now gets its own board kind instead of rendering as
  TURN_ENDED or ERROR. Nothing about the item, the worktree or the work is wrong — the provider
  declined to run, and a rework or an auto-continue would just be refused again. BOSS: until now a
  quota refusal was invisible on the board and showed only in the provider's own log.

  RESTART. `zsh dispatcherctl.sh restart` died with `command not found`: line 156 called "$0", which
  is a bare relative name when the script is invoked that way. SELF=${0:A} resolves a relative name
  AND a symlink; the action and the refusal message both use it.

  WHY IT SURVIVED, which is the more useful half: the --dry-run branch exits BEFORE the
  re-invocation, so the only branch anyone could exercise without stopping the daemon was the one
  that never reaches the bug. The guard was tested and the action was not — the same class ruled on
  tonight, in the form where the proof set simply never enters the room. The fix therefore carries a
  seam: `selfpath` prints exactly the path restart would re-invoke, and the new test runs it the way
  BOSS ran it (bare relative name, unrelated cwd) and requires a real file back.

  Mutations 6 of 6 CAUGHT, sources restored and md5-verified: drop the stagger; mark a deferred item
  broken; start the stagger clock on attempt; let a refusal render as a turn end; revert restart to a
  bare $0; fix only the message and leave the action broken.

  A FIXTURE DEFECT CAUGHT BY THE STAGGER ITSELF: the rotation checks dispatch several times in the
  same second, so with staggering on they were deferred and passed for the wrong reason — proving
  the stagger rather than the route. Staggering is now off in the base config and exercised on its
  own.

2026-09-08 06:1x  SESSION AFFINITY — place once, then leave it alone (WORKER-2)
  museadapter.py, dispatcher.py, tests/test_muse.py (117), tests/test_codexroute.py (37),
  MUSE_ADAPTER.md. Suite ALL GREEN.

  BOSS withdrew the per-launch rotation an hour after asking for it, and he was right to: prompt
  cache is per ACCOUNT and is 92% of everything Muse consumes (Go plan, 8,314,573,726 cache-read
  against 4,988,483 output). Moving a live conversation to another key throws its prefix away and
  re-sends it at full price, so distribution and cache pull in opposite directions.

  His own check found the gap that made this urgent: select_profile's rule 1 honoured
  item["profile"] and NOTHING EVER WROTE IT. The chosen profile lived only in
  self.state["codex"][slot] — slot-keyed RUN state — so a resume or re-spawn of the same item
  re-entered selection with nothing and was placed again somewhere else.

  Now: a conversation's key is chosen once by LEAST-LOADED placement across the healthy Go profiles,
  written onto the ITEM as muse_profile, and read back on every later dispatch. Least-loaded rather
  than a cursor: it spreads by the thing that matters (warm conversations already on a key) and
  self-corrects after a re-placement. Affinity follows the CONVERSATION, never CODEX-N — a
  slot-keyed map moves warm sessions onto cold keys the moment slots are reassigned.

  A warm conversation moves only when waiting cannot help: a ROLLING wall means WAIT (the window
  refills; the cache outlives it), a WEEKLY wall or an AUTH hold means MOVE. An UNRECOGNISED wall
  counts as rolling — guessing rolling idles a slot until someone looks, guessing weekly burns a
  warm prefix on every wall we cannot parse, and the asymmetry is not close.

  account_load() reports conversations per key, because ten sticky sessions over three accounts is
  3-4 warm conversations per key and a weekly wall now takes 3-4 of them down together.

  A MISSED MUTATION, and what it found. Ignoring the wall's scope in the REAP scored MISSED: every
  check drove the SPAWN, and the scope is parsed in the reap — a guard on a path nothing in the
  proof set entered, the same shape as the restart bug an hour earlier. Adding reap-level checks
  then found a REAL DEFECT: `weekly limit reached` matched no QUOTA pattern at all, so a weekly
  refusal was not classified as a refusal — no hold, no REFUSED row, and the next tick dispatched
  straight back into the same wall. Fixed and pinned.

  Mutations after that: 9 of 9 CAUGHT across both files (ignore affinity; move for a rolling wall;
  wait for a weekly wall; default an unknown wall to weekly; place on the head instead of
  least-loaded; stop writing affinity onto the item; treat WAIT as an ordinary selection; ignore the
  scope in the reap; drop `limit reached`). One earlier attempt produced a NameError rather than a
  verdict and was re-run as a valid mutation instead of being counted — a crash is neither a catch
  nor a miss.


2026-09-08 06:3x  Live HANDLES stripped from Muse workers — my defect, found in the list (WORKER-2)
  museadapter.py, tests/test_muse.py (119). Suite ALL GREEN.

  BOSS and ★ listed the running daemon's environment (names only) and found SSH_AUTH_SOCK alongside
  all six OPENCODE keys. I checked my own stripping against it and it does NOT remove it:
  SSH_AUTH_SOCK matches none of KEY/TOKEN/SECRET/PASSWORD/CREDENTIAL and none of the prefixes. So
  the Muse path was carrying the agent socket too, in code I had told BOSS was clean.

  A handle is a different category from a secret. A secret is INFORMATION — holding it lets someone
  act as us later. A handle is AUTHORITY — a live connection to something that acts on the owner's
  behalf on request. A process holding SSH_AUTH_SOCK can ask the owner's running agent to SIGN: it
  never sees the private key, it is not limited to reading, and the agent does not ask who is
  calling. That is push access to everything the agent can authenticate.

  HANDLES now strips SSH_AUTH_SOCK, SSH_AGENT_PID, GPG_AGENT_INFO, DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS,
  CLAUDE_CODE_MESSAGING_SOCKET, DOCKER_HOST and KUBECONFIG. 2 of 2 mutations CAUGHT.

  THE LESSON IS BIGGER THAN THE LIST: a deny-list by pattern removes exactly what somebody thought
  to name, and what nobody named is indistinguishable from what is not there. The list is now
  enumerated and commented rather than inferred from word-shapes.

  MUSE PATH ONLY. The LEGACY branch of route_flags still passes the unstripped environment to every
  Astra spawn — the owner's decision, with both recommendations attached, and it is the same
  two-line change pointing that branch at child_env. Stripping a socket from workers I introduced is
  not a behaviour change to anything that was working; changing what the owner's reviewer slots
  receive is.


2026-09-08 06:5x  THE DEGRADED TICK — codex unblocked, and the do-nothing path made to say so
  dispatcher.py, tests/test_serverdown.py (NEW, 11), tests/run_all.sh. Suite 65 files, ALL GREEN.

  BOSS found why ZERO Muse workers have ever run under the daemon, and it was none of the things we
  had been fixing. The tick pings /global/health on the opencode server and returns early on any
  exception — before codex_tick and before every dispatch path. The opencode server has been down
  since 2026-09-07 13:13:42, so the tick has returned early on every iteration for ~16 hours.

  Codex and Muse workers have NO functional dependency on that server. They are `codex exec`
  subprocesses on another provider. They were blocked by a health check for a service they never
  use, which means the routing, the wiring, the restarts and the config were all correct and all
  irrelevant.

  1. DECOUPLED. codex_only_pass() runs the codex half on a tick that is returning early for
     opencode's sake: load the queue, run codex_tick, write it back if it changed. Deliberately
     minimal, and wrapped so a failure in the degraded path cannot take the daemon with it.

  2. THE CONDITION IS RE-ASSERTED EVERY TICK. SERVER_DOWN stays one-shot — an event is a statement
     about a moment — but state["degraded"] now carries reason, since, for_s, effect and as_of, and
     is rewritten on every degraded tick and CLEARED on the first healthy one. Sixteen hours of
     outage had been announced once, yesterday, and were silent afterwards: a recurring condition
     rendered as a single historical event.

  3. THREE STATES, NOT ONE STRING. "idle: no eligible CODEX item" was covering an empty queue, a
     refused/deferred spawn, and a tick that never reached the code. refuse_spawn() now records a
     reason at all nine non-spawning exits from codex_spawn, so the slot's line says which.

  AND THE LIVENESS SIGNALS THEMSELVES. save_state() ran before the return and the heartbeat was
  written at the TOP of the tick, so state.json was fresh, the heartbeat was seconds old, and every
  signal on the board was being written BY THE CODE PATH THAT DOES NOTHING — a daemon that could do
  no work was indistinguishable from a healthy idle one, for sixteen hours. write_heartbeat(degraded)
  now stamps the reason into the heartbeat itself, which both readers already print.

  Mutations 6 of 6 CAUGHT (sources restored and md5-verified): restore the original early return;
  write a clean heartbeat on the degraded path; freeze the duration after the first assertion; never
  clear the mark on recovery; collapse the three idle states. One attempt produced a KeyError rather
  than a verdict — the checks now read state defensively so a removed field fails a CHECK instead of
  raising, and it was re-scored as a valid mutation.

  A FIXTURE DEFECT, the sixth tonight: `spawned = []` between ticks REBOUND the name while the
  daemon's stub still held the old list, so the second tick's dispatch landed where the check could
  not see it and failed for a reason unrelated to the daemon. Cleared in place now.

  I did NOT start the opencode server, per BOSS: it would revive fourteen EXEC sessions dead for
  sixteen hours, mid-task, with stale state — a large side effect to route around a small bug.

  Addendum, same change: two fixtures build a Dispatcher without running __init__ (test_codexroute
  via __new__, test_observe via a Stub that overrides it), so `_spawn_refusal` had to be created in
  each of them. Deliberately NOT defaulted defensively in the product — making codex_spawn tolerate
  a half-built object would hide exactly the missing initialisation a test exists to find. The whole
  suite caught both; my own new file did not, because it had set the field itself.
